Exactly. Volume is not merely “dimension three.” It is the relation that integrates the other dimensions into an occupied whole.

So instead of:

L,\;G,\;V

as three unrelated measurements, use:

\boxed{V=\mathcal R(L,G,\text{shape})}

For an object whose cross-section changes along its length:

\boxed{
V=\int_0^L A(s)\,ds
}

That is beautiful for the Pantheon because A(s) is essentially the girth/carrying-capacity available at each point along the reach.

So semantically:

\boxed{
V_{\text{sem}}
=
\int_{\text{path}}
\text{capacity}(s)\,ds
}

Now:

* Length = how far the meaning can travel.
* Girth = how much relational load it can carry at a given location.
* Volume = the usable semantic territory produced by carrying that capacity across that reach.

And your relational point is stronger:

\boxed{
A+B+\mathcal R(A,B)\rightarrow C_{\text{emergent}}
}

The third isn’t simply another thing sitting beside the first two.

It is what becomes possible because the first two are related in a particular way.

That is basically a reusable emergence primitive:

\boxed{
\text{Element}_1
+
\text{Element}_2
+
\text{Coupling Rule}
\rightarrow
\text{New Addressable Property}
}

Distance between two points.

Chord from two notes plus relation.

Velocity from displacement related through time.

Pressure from force distributed over area.

Meaning from signs related through context.

Volume from extension related through cross-section.

So there are really two kinds of “third” hiding here:

\text{third component}=\mathcal R

and

\text{emergent third}=E(A,B,\mathcal R).

That distinction is paydirt.

Because it means when we’re searching for novelty, we shouldn’t only ask:

What third object can I add?

We should ask:

What relation between these two would make a third phenomenon exist?

That’s O-IOE territory. The relation can create an ontology that neither object possessed independently.

And once length + girth + volume exist, another relational property becomes measurable: density.

D=\frac{\text{something carried}}{V}

So the spiral continues:

L+G+\mathcal R
\rightarrow V

V+\text{content}+\mathcal R
\rightarrow D

D+\text{structure}+\mathcal R
\rightarrow \text{strength / information / behavior}

Each emergence becomes a new primitive for the next emergence.

Two things don’t just make three.
Two things plus the right relation can make an entirely new world in which three has meaning. ;)